The anatomy, verified:

```js
// CUSTOM HOOK = function starting with 'use' + calls hooks inside
function useDebouncedValue(value, delay = 300) {
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);
  useEffect(() => {
    const t = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), delay);
    return () => clearTimeout(t);   // CLEANUP: the previous timer dies on each change
  }, [value, delay]);
  return debounced;                  // stable return: just the value
}

// USE: each caller gets its OWN state — no sharing
function Search() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  const debounced = useDebouncedValue(query, 300);
  useEffect(() => { if (debounced) search(debounced); }, [debounced]);
  // typing 'react' fires ONE search 300ms after the last keystroke (verified)
}
```

The rules it inherits (because it calls hooks):
- Only call at the top level of components or other hooks — never conditional, never in loops
- Name starts with `use` — this is also how the lint plugin enforces the rules for it

What a custom hook is NOT (the misconceptions to correct):
- NOT state sharing — two components calling useToggle() have independent toggles (verified: separate slot per component). Shared state needs lifting, context, or a store.
- NOT a component — no JSX, no render; it's logic extraction
- NOT free structure — the return shape is your API: a tuple [value, setter] for pair-shaped like useState, an object for multiple named results ({ data, loading, error } for data hooks)

The quality markers (what 'good' means):
1. Handles the full lifecycle: loading + error + cleanup (the unmount race: a fetch resolving after unmount must not setState — guard with an ignore flag or AbortController)
2. Stable references: memoize returned functions (useCallback) so callers' deps don't churn
3. Configurable via arguments, opinionated via defaults
